      "id": 1,
      "text": "Little Haiti, officially named in 2016, traces its roots back to the historic Lemon City founded by Bahamian immigrants before Miami existed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Haiti",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A 13‑foot bronze statue of General Toussaint L'Ouverture stands on N. Miami Avenue. and 62nd St., celebrating Haiti’s revolutionary history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Haiti",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Little Haiti’s streets pulse with French‑Creole culture, featuring restaurants, art galleries, dance, music, theatre, and family‑owned businesses that reflect Caribbean heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Haiti",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The neighborhood is home to Haitian immigrants and residents from the wider Caribbean, making it a vibrant hub of Afro‑Caribbean culture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
